The Theorem-to-Turbine Gap

A Systems Engineering Critique of Mathematical Abundance, or Why the Physical Universe Does Not Accept LaTeX as Legal Tender

Abstract

On 21 September 2026, OpenAI stated that an internal model had resolved more than 100 long-standing open mathematical problems across most areas of mathematics, in addition to its previously announced resolution of the Navier–Stokes Millennium Prize problem. OpenAI described mathematics as a fundamental science whose novel discoveries “may result in a wide range of applications.” 

The word may is doing considerable engineering work.

This paper examines a category error increasingly visible in discussion of artificial intelligence and scientific progress: the implicit compression of mathematical discovery, scientific understanding, engineering development, industrial production and economic deployment into a single phenomenon called “progress.” They are not the same phenomenon.

Mathematical capability can expand the space of known or theoretically possible solutions. It cannot, by itself, supply capital, factories, skilled labour, raw materials, energy, certification, manufacturing yield, logistics, maintenance infrastructure or customers. Nuclear fusion provides a useful example. The basic fusion reaction is understood, yet organisations attempting to turn it into commercial electricity still confront materials degradation, heat extraction, tritium production, maintenance, fuel supply, manufacturing and economic problems. ITER and the US Department of Energy explicitly identify these as major remaining barriers. 

The central argument is therefore simple: an increase in mathematical output is not equivalent to an increase in deployable human capability. Indeed, sufficiently rapid AI-generated discovery may produce the opposite bottleneck from the one commonly imagined. If ideas become abundant while capital, manufacturing capacity and physical resources remain scarce, civilisation acquires not an infinite technology pipeline but an increasingly severe selection problem.

Or, stated formally:

\boxed{\text{Proof} \neq \text{Product}}

