1. Introduction: Congratulations on Solving Mathematics

There is something undeniably funny about watching adults discuss mathematics as though somebody has discovered the administrator password for reality.

An artificial intelligence system solves a difficult theorem and the discussion rapidly moves through a familiar sequence:

\text{better mathematics}
\rightarrow
\text{better science}
\rightarrow
\text{better technology}
\rightarrow
\text{economic abundance}
\rightarrow
\text{civilisational transformation}.

Each arrow is treated almost as punctuation.

Unfortunately, each arrow contains an entire industry.

OpenAI’s September 2026 announcement is genuinely remarkable on its stated terms. The company says a newly trained internal model has resolved more than 100 long-standing mathematical problems spanning much of mathematics. The pace was sufficiently unusual that OpenAI established an independent advisory group of mathematicians to help assess significance, dissemination and professional standards surrounding future results. 

That is an important development in automated mathematical reasoning.

It is not 100 factories.

It is not 100 power stations.

It is not 100 pharmaceutical manufacturing lines.

It is not 100 functioning transport networks.

It is not even necessarily 100 useful technologies.

Those things may eventually follow from some mathematical discoveries. But between a theorem and a turbine lies a region apparently missing from certain conceptions of technological progress.

Engineers know this region well.

It is where the money goes.

