2. The Infinite Money Tree Lemma

Consider a simplified model of technological deployment.

Let

M = \text{mathematical knowledge},

S = \text{scientific understanding},

E = \text{engineering capability},

C = \text{available capital},

R = \text{physical resources},

F = \text{manufacturing capability},

L = \text{logistical capacity},

W = \text{skilled workforce},

G = \text{regulatory and institutional capacity},

and

D = \text{economically deployable output}.

The popular mythology effectively assumes

D=f(M),

with sufficiently advanced mathematics eventually producing sufficiently advanced civilisation.

A physical economy behaves more like

D=f(M,S,E,C,R,F,L,W,G,\ldots).

Worse, many real engineering systems behave approximately as bottleneck systems.

One might therefore represent deployment schematically as

D \lesssim
\min
\left(
\alpha_M M,
\alpha_S S,
\alpha_E E,
\alpha_C C,
\alpha_R R,
\alpha_F F,
\alpha_L L,
\alpha_W W,
\alpha_G G
\right).

The equation is deliberately schematic rather than an empirical production function. Its purpose is to illustrate a basic systems property:

making a non-limiting input effectively infinite does not necessarily increase system output at all.

If you possess the complete mathematical design for a bridge but have no steel, you do not possess a bridge.

If you possess perfect aerodynamic equations but no manufacturing process capable of holding the required tolerances, you do not possess an aircraft.

If your artificial intelligence invents a revolutionary battery requiring a material available globally at six kilograms per year, congratulations on your new PDF.

This yields the Infinite Money Tree Lemma.

Lemma

For a physical technology,

M\rightarrow\infty

does not imply

D\rightarrow\infty.

Proof

Attempt to pay a steel supplier with a theorem.

\square

