3. Unfortunately, Matter Has Terms and Conditions

Mathematics enjoys several properties that physical engineering very much does not.

A mathematical object does not become more expensive because 400 researchers wish to inspect it simultaneously.

A proof does not require a replacement bearing.

A theorem does not suffer thermal expansion.

A lemma does not arrive six months late because a specialist casting supplier in Germany has entered administration.

Nobody has ever rejected a differential equation because the weld penetration was inadequate.

Reality imposes additional conditions.

A technology must generally be:

* physically possible;
* controllable;
* manufacturable;
* reliable;
* maintainable;
* repairable;
* safe enough;
* insurable;
* certifiable;
* supplied;
* affordable;
* scalable;
* and worth somebody paying for.

The difference between something working in principle and working as infrastructure is enormous.

Engineering history is full of systems whose underlying principles were understood decades before widespread deployment became practical.

This is not evidence that science failed.

It is evidence that the rest of the process exists.

