4. Fusion: The Universe Refuses to Read the Paper

Nuclear fusion is almost comically well suited to illustrating the theorem-to-turbine gap.

Humanity does not need a mathematician to discover that deuterium and tritium can fuse.

We know.

The reaction can be written down rather compactly.

The difficulty begins approximately five seconds after somebody says:

“Excellent. Now turn it into a power station.”

At that point the problem changes character.

A useful fusion plant has to achieve and integrate, among other things:

plasma confinement, sustained operation, heat handling, neutron-resistant materials, fuel-cycle management, tritium breeding and recovery, heat extraction, electricity generation, remote maintenance, component replacement, acceptable plant availability, safety compliance, construction feasibility and competitive economics.

ITER itself identifies resistant materials, divertor heat exhaust, remote handling, large-scale tritium production and recycling, and efficient conversion of heat into electricity among the significant remaining challenges between fusion experimentation and practical power generation. 

The materials problem alone is unpleasant. High-energy fusion neutrons damage structural materials through atomic displacement and transmutation, degrading material properties over time. 

Then there is fuel.

Tritium does not conveniently occur on Earth in the quantities required for a large commercial fusion sector. Future deuterium–tritium plants are therefore expected to require fuel-cycle infrastructure capable of producing and managing it. The US Department of Energy explicitly describes robust isotope supply chains and tritium breeding as requirements that must be addressed before widespread commercial fusion deployment. 

None of these statements imply that fusion cannot work.

They demonstrate something more mundane and more important:

knowing the physics is not identical to possessing the industrial system.

The hypothetical conversation therefore proceeds:

Mathematician: We have improved the governing model.

Physicist: Excellent. Plasma performance improves considerably.

Engineer: Brilliant. What are we making the first wall from?

Mathematician: The governing model.

Engineer: Right.

Eventually the procurement department enters the room and destroys everybody’s remaining optimism with a spreadsheet.

