5. Theorem ≠ Prototype ≠ Product ≠ Infrastructure

Technological discussion often collapses several fundamentally different stages.

A more useful chain is:

\text{Mathematical result}

\downarrow

\text{Scientific interpretation}

\downarrow

\text{Engineering concept}

\downarrow

\text{Prototype}

\downarrow

\text{Validated prototype}

\downarrow

\text{Manufacturable system}

\downarrow

\text{Economically viable product}

\downarrow

\text{Scaled industrial deployment}.

Every arrow can kill the project.

The mathematical result can be perfectly correct while the resulting device is economically useless.

The engineering concept can work while proving impossible to mass manufacture.

The prototype can perform brilliantly while requiring £800,000 of hand assembly.

The commercial product can function while customers refuse to buy it.

The customers can want it while the electricity grid cannot connect it.

The infrastructure can be buildable while financing costs make the project uneconomic.

Nothing in mathematics repeals these constraints.

