6. The Bruce Lee Problem

A maxim commonly attributed to Bruce Lee says, in effect, that the dangerous fighter is not the person who has practised ten thousand kicks once, but the person who has practised one kick ten thousand times.

There is an engineering version.

An intelligence capable of producing 10,000 novel designs is impressive.

A company capable of forcing one design through 10,000 miserable iterations until it survives contact with reality may still be more economically consequential.

Prototype 1 works.

Prototype 2 leaks.

Prototype 4 overheats.

Prototype 11 solves the overheating and becomes impossible to manufacture.

Prototype 19 can be manufactured but costs twelve times the target price.

Prototype 34 reduces the cost but introduces vibration.

Prototype 61 passes the laboratory test and fails outdoors.

Prototype 103 works outdoors and corrodes.

Prototype 141 solves the corrosion and violates a certification requirement.

Prototype 208 passes certification.

Prototype 209 reveals that the supplier of one critical component can only make 300 units annually.

Prototype 264 finally works.

Then purchasing asks whether the entire thing could be 17% cheaper.

This process looks intellectually unimpressive compared with solving an open theorem.

It is also how much of the built world comes into existence.

Civilisation depends extensively upon people repeatedly doing boring things slightly better.

