7. Idea Abundance Does Not Produce Resource Abundance

Suppose future AI systems become absurdly productive.

In a year they generate:

10,000 viable battery concepts.

3,000 reactor architectures.

50,000 new materials.

6,000 propulsion systems.

80,000 medical hypotheses.

100,000 manufacturing improvements.

Assume, generously, that all of these are legitimate advances.

What then?

You have not generated 149,000 engineering organisations.

You have not generated their prototype budgets.

You have not created laboratory space.

You have not multiplied the population of experienced machinists.

You have not conjured specialist metals out of vacuum.

You have not automatically enlarged electricity grids, ports, fabs, mines, test facilities or regulatory organisations.

You have generated options.

That matters.

But an option is not an installation.

Indeed, if discovery becomes cheap enough, the economic problem may invert.

Historically:

\text{ideas scarce} \quad\rightarrow\quad \text{search for ideas}.

Under extreme AI capability:

\text{ideas abundant} \quad\rightarrow\quad \text{search for which ideas deserve reality}.

That is not the elimination of scarcity.

It is the relocation of scarcity.

