8. The Great Civilisational Queue

Imagine that an AI tomorrow produces one million legitimate technical improvements.

The world cannot implement one million major industrial programmes simultaneously.

Projects therefore enter a queue.

Some will receive capital.

Some will not.

Some will receive engineers.

Some will not.

Some will compete for the same materials.

Some will conflict with existing infrastructure.

Some will fail environmental assessments.

Some will depend upon manufacturing capacity that does not yet exist.

Some will require ten years of testing.

Some will be technically superior to existing products but commercially inferior because replacement costs are too high.

And some will simply lose to another idea promising investors a better return.

The universe has not become post-scarcity.

It has acquired a much larger backlog.

