9. Capital: The Variable That Keeps Being Left Out

There is a strangely persistent habit in technology discourse of treating capital as though it is merely permission.

It is not.

Capital represents claims on scarce productive resources.

A £5 billion reactor project consumes real things:

engineering hours, steel, concrete, specialist manufacturing equipment, transport, energy, land, project management, insurance, regulatory work and years of human effort.

If ten projects require the same finite manufacturing capacity, mathematics cannot permit all ten to occupy it simultaneously.

Money cannot fully repeal this constraint either.

Increase demand for a genuinely scarce input and one likely result is simply that its price rises.

Hence another useful distinction:

\text{technically desirable}
\neq
\text{economically financeable}.

And:

\text{economically financeable}
\neq
\text{socially optimal}.

Capital allocation is not an automatic ranking of human usefulness.

Projects are selected through expected returns, risk, time horizons, policy, ownership structures, financing conditions and market demand.

A technology capable of improving millions of lives can remain underfunded.

A trivial consumer product can receive billions.

No theorem changes the incentive structure simply by existing.

