10. The Monetisation Filter

This leads to a slightly uncomfortable conclusion.

If AI dramatically expands the set of technically possible things, deployment will still be disproportionately attracted toward the subset that somebody can monetise.

The filter is not:

\text{Does humanity benefit?}

The practical filter is often closer to:

\text{Can somebody capture enough of the benefit to justify paying for it?}

Those are not equivalent questions.

A mathematical advance may generate enormous public value while offering no obvious proprietary revenue model.

Another may enable a product with modest social importance but enormous consumer demand.

The second can receive capital first.

This is not a mathematical failure.

It is an economic selection mechanism.

AI may make discovery more abundant without changing that mechanism at all.

