11. Ten Thousand Inventions, One Machine Shop

Here the physical absurdity becomes clearest.

Imagine an AI discovers ten thousand brilliant new machines on Monday.

On Tuesday, all ten thousand teams telephone the same precision-manufacturing company.

The company owns six suitable five-axis machines.

The AI has revolutionised invention.

The lead time is now fourteen months.

This is not an edge case.

Modern industrial capability depends upon dense networks of specialised suppliers, expertise and equipment.

Increasing demand for those systems faster than their capacity expands creates congestion.

So an intelligence explosion need not initially resemble:

\text{intelligence}\uparrow
\Rightarrow
\text{output}\uparrow\uparrow\uparrow.

It can produce:

\text{intelligence}\uparrow\uparrow\uparrow

while

\text{machine tools}\uparrow

and consequently

\text{queue length}\uparrow\uparrow\uparrow.

At some point somebody will presumably publish a theorem proving that the machine shop should buy another machine.

The machine will still take nine months to arrive.

