13. AI May Make Engineering More Important, Not Less

There is a counterintuitive possibility here.

Suppose AI eventually makes mathematical derivation and theoretical exploration extremely cheap.

The relative value of activities it cannot cheaply replace rises.

Experimental validation.

Instrumentation.

Metrology.

Manufacturing.

Field testing.

Maintenance.

Supply-chain construction.

Operations.

Regulatory evidence.

Capital deployment.

Physical troubleshooting.

The engineer standing beside a £20 million machine saying “that bearing should not sound like that” has not been intellectually defeated by the theorem prover.

They are operating at a different interface with reality.

The more cheaply ideas can be generated, the more valuable reliable mechanisms for killing bad ideas quickly may become.

The great technological capability of the AI era may therefore not merely be invention.

It may be disciplined rejection.

Given one million plausible possibilities, identify the 100 worth prototyping.

Given 100 prototypes, identify the ten worth industrialising.

Given ten industrial systems, identify the two worth scaling.

That is not an embarrassment caused by scarcity.

That is what allocation under scarcity means.

⸻

14. A New Law of AI Economics

We may therefore propose the Law of Uncooperative Reality:

As the marginal cost of generating technically plausible ideas approaches zero, the relative importance of physical verification, resource allocation and deployment constraints approaches one.

Or, less politely:

Once ideas become cheap, reality becomes the expensive bit.

There is no contradiction here.

AI might enormously accelerate human progress.

It might solve mathematical problems at a rate no human institution can approach.

It might expose new physical principles, materials and designs.

But those achievements increase the number of possible futures.

They do not automatically select, finance and construct one.

