15. Please Proceed to the Loading Bay

The mistake is not excitement about mathematical progress.

The mistake is confusing epistemic progress with deployed capability.

OpenAI itself chose careful wording when it wrote that mathematical discoveries “may result in a wide range of applications.” 

Precisely.

May.

Between may and does sits engineering.

Between does and scales sits industry.

Between scales and benefits humanity sits economics, institutions and politics.

A civilisation cannot eat mathematical possibility.

It cannot heat houses with benchmark scores.

It cannot manufacture aircraft from inference tokens.

It cannot build a fusion reactor from confidence intervals.

The proof can tell us that something is possible.

Science can tell us what physical mechanism might achieve it.

Engineering can attempt to construct it.

Manufacturing can attempt to reproduce it.

Capital can attempt to finance it.

Logistics can attempt to supply it.

Markets and states can decide whether anyone actually deploys it.

Only after that does somebody get to plug the bloody thing in.

So by all means solve 100 mathematical problems.

Solve 100,000.

Solve every theorem humanity has ever written down.

That would be an extraordinary intellectual achievement.

But when the applause finishes, somebody still has to walk into a workshop, order the material, design the tooling, build the prototype, break the prototype, work out why it broke, build another one, persuade somebody to fund it, manufacture it economically, get permission to operate it, establish a supply chain, train people to maintain it and find somebody willing to buy the result.

The physical universe remains stubbornly unimpressed by PowerPoint.

And, regrettably for everyone involved,

\boxed{\text{Proof}\neq\text{Product}.}

